{
  "image_name": "0d94f980479d07fa295e5f340b61b281.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure contrasts pain signaling in a healthy joint with an osteoarthritic (OA) joint. In the healthy state, mechanical, noxious, chemical, or electrical stimuli activate joint nociceptors, and the signal travels through the dorsal root ganglion and spinal cord to the brain. Neurotransmitters such as substance P and CGRP are shown at the sensory neuron terminals, indicating normal pain transmission.\n\nIn OA, tissue injury in the joint appears to amplify this pathway. The damaged joint activates nociceptors more strongly, leading not only to pain transmission but also to peripheral sensitization. A zoomed inset suggests that sensory fibers release substance P and CGRP locally, while NGF acting through TrkA promotes inflammatory responses, including vasodilation, inflammation, chemotaxis, and cell aggregation. This indicates that the nerve endings are contributing to local joint inflammation as well as sensing pain.\n\nThe lower pathway also shows antidrromic transport and enhanced signaling in the spinal cord, where substance P and CGRP are again involved. Overall, the figure presents OA pain as a feed-forward process in which joint injury increases both peripheral inflammation and central sensitization, producing more persistent and amplified pain than in a healthy joint.",
